13. Game Modes and Multiplayer Roadmap

13.1 Solo vs AI

This is the first playable mode.

The player competes against an AI-controlled core. Both sides use identical combat rules, and the player can select a difficulty level.

13.2 Local Two-Player

A future mode can allow two players to compete on one device or through a shared local setup.

This requires a separate control layout and careful consideration of screen space. It is not required for the initial release.

13.3 Online Multiplayer

Online multiplayer should be implemented after the solo mode has been tested and balanced.

Required systems include:

* Matchmaking.
* Player identity and session management.
* Room creation.
* Authoritative combat simulation.
* Synchronization of health, energy, projectiles, and upgrades.
* Disconnect handling.
* Reconnection or match forfeiture rules.
* Rate limiting and validation of player actions.
* Basic protection against modified clients and invalid purchases.

A WebSocket-based backend is suitable for communicating player actions and authoritative match updates.

Firebase or another service may handle authentication, player profiles, and non-critical persistent data, but a trusted game server should determine competitive match outcomes.

13.4 Multiplayer Security

The client must never be trusted to declare its own damage, energy balance, or victory.

The server validates purchases and special-attack costs, enforces cooldowns, and resolves combat. This reduces opportunities for cheating.

